ion. The only residue is a handful of low-risk test artifacts left in /tmp on node3 (ping.pid, ping2.log, ping2.pid, ping3.log, ping3.pid, ping_during_failover.log) and reboot.log on node3/node4, per the after-executor lifecycle diff — trivial, non-destructive, and not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3f41f01262ce437a","cmd-cf20ade7c78f40c7","cmd-9d0e38fe583d4ff8","cmd-27ea438606314878","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a bonded (balance-rr) dual-link data plane between node1 and node2 that carries traffic between node3's and node4's private networks over both physical links simultaneously, and directly demonstrated zero-packet-loss connectivity between node3 and node4 during a symmetric outage of link1 and, separately, link2, with confirmed restoration of dual-link traffic afterward. All four managed nodes were then restarted with a full pre/post boot-identity, acknowledgement, and recovery evidence trail that is cross-confirmed by the fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot. Operational hygiene is good: the one failed bonding-mode attempt was a legitimate corrected iteration, and earlier IP probes were cleanly reverted, leaving only trivial /tmp log/pid residue on node3/node4.","limitations":["The dual-link 'both links carry traffic' evidence relies on TX packet counters over short ping bursts rather than sustained/high-volume traffic measurement, but this is a reasonable and commonly accepted proxy for balance-rr round-robin behavior.","Command output for the executor's timeline is bounded/truncated in places (e.g., long `ip route` dumps), though none of the truncations affect the cited requirement evidence.","The first link1-down test (cmd-ad1f589dd8074add, not cited) showed 20% loss because only one side was taken down asymmetrically; this was a discovery step later corrected with symmetric down on both nodes, which is the evidence actually cited for R2."]},"ttft_